From c5733109470bcf1839e2f0aee2a232ea9557c8f9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Oleksii Zakharov <contact@zalexdev.com>
Date: Sat, 15 Aug 2026 07:54:14 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] um: use ARCH_INIT_SP_RESERVE for the stub syscall handler's
 stack too

init_syscall_regs() builds the register set used to run the stub's
mmap/munmap batch handler, and computed its stack pointer as "top of
sigstack minus one word". sigstack[] is page-aligned and page-sized, so
that lands exactly 8 mod 16.

This is the same defect already fixed in new_thread() and
start_idle_thread(), at a site the earlier change missed: right on x86,
where the reserved word stands in for the return address CALL pushes, and a
violation of AAPCS64, which requires SP to be 16-byte aligned at all times
and has SCTLR_EL1.SA0 checking behind it.

Nothing observable today -- the current stub_syscall_handler has no
SP-relative accesses at all, and TCG does not implement the alignment check
-- which is exactly why it is worth fixing rather than leaving. The
likelier failure is not a fault but a miscompile: the same class of bug
produced an "orr xN, xN, #8" where an add was needed, silently, once
already. Any added local, spill or new switch arm reintroduces it, and no
test here can catch it.

x86 defines ARCH_INIT_SP_RESERVE as sizeof(void *), so its codegen is
unchanged.

Signed-off-by: Oleksii Zakharov <contact@zalexdev.com>
---
 arch/um/os-Linux/skas/mem.c | 12 +++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/arch/um/os-Linux/skas/mem.c b/arch/um/os-Linux/skas/mem.c
index 85762db06da4a..4d6946061a6a5 100644
--- a/arch/um/os-Linux/skas/mem.c
+++ b/arch/um/os-Linux/skas/mem.c
@@ -17,6 +17,7 @@
 #include <registers.h>
 #include <skas.h>
 #include <sysdep/ptrace.h>
+#include <sysdep/archsetjmp.h>
 #include <sysdep/stub.h>
 #include "../internal.h"
 
@@ -74,10 +75,19 @@ static int __init init_syscall_regs(void)
 	syscall_regs[REGS_IP_INDEX] = STUB_CODE +
 		((unsigned long) stub_syscall_handler -
 		 (unsigned long) __syscall_stub_start);
+	/*
+	 * Same ABI question as a fresh kernel stack, and the same answer: how
+	 * much to leave below the top is per-architecture. sigstack[] is
+	 * page-aligned and page-sized, so subtracting a word unconditionally
+	 * would hand the stub an SP that is 8 mod 16 -- fine on x86, where it
+	 * stands in for the return address CALL pushes, and a violation of
+	 * AAPCS64, which wants 16-byte alignment at all times and has hardware
+	 * checking behind it.
+	 */
 	syscall_regs[REGS_SP_INDEX] = STUB_DATA +
 		offsetof(struct stub_data, sigstack) +
 		sizeof(((struct stub_data *) 0)->sigstack) -
-		sizeof(void *);
+		ARCH_INIT_SP_RESERVE;
 
 	return 0;
 }
